    "q-161695": {
      "dom_id": "step-q161695",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14209435/assets/214d629cdac48642a7a8b4d435f91098388a7a799bd7e70d5b7c91ae028714c3.png)\n\nFind the volume of the solid region above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14209435/assets/bf9c4790a8e31e000d2aeb7f7e557661683d3f52d737ba1b99abd407c15d6e99.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type I region\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3+x-y\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14209435/assets/74954715928551ebb271cdfd461d783778b02b7944cbfd5502d6d06011408ad8.png)\n\nHence, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3+x-y\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3+x-y}dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=3+x-y}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(3+x-y)-0\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3+x-y\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[(3+x)y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}((3+x)(2)-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2})-0\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}4+2x\\,dx \\\\  & =[4x+{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(8+4)-0 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
